ies exist; the eight\nE001 → adoption edges are justified by Rule 12 (framework → consumer) and the eight\nadoption → E010 edges by Rule 11 (docs last); the strategy's statement that adoptions\nare independent of each other once the convention exists is honored (no\nadoption → adoption edges, so parallel work is not serialized). Critical path length\n3 is reasonable given the strategy's explicit fan-out/fan-in shape.\n\nHowever, the diagram/frontmatter cross-check fails: E010's frontmatter lists\nRHAISTRAT-9011-E001 in its dependencies (9 entries: E001-E009), and the summary's\nDAG Justification table claims \"E001..E009 → E010\", but the mermaid diagram in the\nDependency DAG section draws only E002 --> E010 through E009 --> E010 — there is no\nE001 --> E010 edge. Every other edge matches frontmatter exactly; this is the sole\nmismatch, but under the cross-check rule a frontmatter dependency with no\ncorresponding diagram edge is a critical issue (the diagram is misleading even though\nthe frontmatter is correct), so this criterion scores 0. The fix is one line: add\n`E001 --> E010` to the diagram (the edge itself is valid and required — E001 is an\nImplementation epic and Rule 11 gates docs-authoring on all implementations).\n\nNo konflux-onboarding epics exist, so the rule 7/8/10 parallelism checks are\nvacuous. No branch files, so the branch/gated_by checks are vacuous. No image-digest,\nRELATED_IMAGE, or endpoint-URL edges. No data/artifact dependency discoverable in\nepic content is missing from the DAG: each adoption consumes only the convention\npublished by E001, which is captured.\n\n### 3. Epic Boundaries (2/2)\nEach epic is a single component/team tuple. Team assignments match the architecture\ncontext owning teams exactly (Dashboard/RHOAI Dashboard, KServe and Serving\nRuntimes/RHOAI Serving, Data Science Pipelines/RHOAI Pipelines, Model Registry/Model\nRegistry, TrustyAI/TrustyAI, Notebooks/Workbenches, Training Operator/Distributed\nWorkloads); Serving Runtimes is corroborated as an RHOAI Serving concern via the\nKServe context's dependency list. E005 bundles the DSPO operator and API server, but\nboth are the same component and team and constitute one logical change (HLR-4 names\nboth) — not a boundary violation. No scope duplication across siblings: each adoption\nepic touches only its own component's logging. No epic mixes code implementation\nwith content authoring (E001 is a spec/convention artifact, E010 is pure\ndocs-authoring). No epic exceeds ~2 weeks. Minor observation, already self-reported\nin Health Warnings: assigning the convention work (E001) to the Documentation\ncomponent is a non-obvious mapping, but it is logged there per the component-name\nconstraint and does not affect execution.\n\n### 4. Type Correctness (2/2)\nNo Investigation epics — justified: the strategy's open questions section states\n\"None\" and the convention epic itself resolves the design unknowns, so no epic's\nexistence or scope hinges on an unresolved question. Every Implementation epic\nproduces a concrete artifact (published convention/schema in E001, code changes in\nE002-E009, published docs topic in E010). E010's implementation_type docs-authoring\nis correct and its gating metadata is clean: no epic sets gated_by, so there are no\ngated_by-without-gate_failure_impact or Investigation-dependency-without-gated_by\ndefects.\n\n### 5. AI Implementability Scoring (1/2)\nAll ten epics are Implementation type and all carry the 9-signal ai_signals field\n(no type/rubric mismatch); all ten ai-signals files exist and their table values\nmatch the frontmatter values exactly. Most values are well justified against the\nrubric and the strategy: E004's external_dependency -1 correctly reflects the\nthird-party serving runtime upstreams (vLLM/OpenVINO/Caikit), E007's adapter_pattern\n0 is properly reasoned (languages unconfirmed), E001's existing_foundation 0 fits a\ngreenfield spec, and open_questions +1 everywhere is consistent with the strategy's\nempty open-questions section. Two arguable values, both on human_process_gates:\n(1) E001 scores 0 with the rationale \"standard peer review, not a formal approval\nboard\", but E001's own acceptance criterion requires review/acceptance recorded from\neach of the seven adopting components' owning teams — a multi-team recorded sign-off\nis a human approval step under the rubric (\"requires human approval step\" → -1),\nand it is decidedly heavier than the standard code review for which E002-E009 scored\n+1; (2) E010's 0 with a thin writer/editor-review rationale is borderline the same\nway given its technical-review AC. Per the rubric's 1-point band (1-2 arguable\nvalues), this criterion scores 1. No value outright contradicts the rubric, and\nai_implementability arithmetic was not checked (computed by the pipeline).\n\n### 6. Acceptance Criteria Quality (2/2)\nEvery epic has testable acceptance criteria derived from the strategy: schema\nvalidation with the five named required fields, unchanged-behavior guarded by the\ncomponent test suite, and for E010 a published topic with example queries plus a\ntechnical-review AC (the rule-mandated docs-authoring review AC is present). No\nkonflux-chain epics exist (no onboarding needed — all components already in the\nplatform), so no build-pipeline AC is required. No replacement of a component occurs\n(log-format changes modify existing components), so rollback/feature-flag ACs are\nnot rule-mandated here. No AC states an external schedule constraint (lab turnaround,\nreview-board cadence, or similar) — nothing needs moving to Risks/Notes. No epic body\nreferences sibling epics by draft ID (E001-E009 appear only in frontmatter\ndependencies, where they are meaningful pre-creation identifiers).\n\n### 7. Completeness (2/2)\nAll strategy scope is accounted for: the convention (HLR-1 → E001), all seven\naffected components have adoption epics (HLR-2..HLR-8), and the user-facing\ndocumentation with example queries (HLR-9 → E010). The problem statement's flagship\nscenario — correlating one user request across Dashboard, serving, and pipelines —\nis carried into E010's ACs (example queries for cross-component correlation) and\nE002's scope (correlation id emission on request handling). Out-of-scope items (log\nstorage/forwarding, metrics/tracing) are correctly excluded. Strategy-level context\nis carried, not dropped: the serving-runtimes upstream risk is reflected in E004's\nscope (configuration-first, midstream/upstream fallback) and the summary's Health\nWarnings and Tiered Delivery; the strategy's missing sections (Impacted Teams, Given/\nWhen/Then, NFRs) are explicitly flagged in Health Warnings rather than silently\nignored. The docs-authoring epic required by documentation support is present (E010).\nNo conditional branches are used, and none are needed — the strategy states no\nbounded-outcome uncertainty.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":32769,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-957693df62b03a5d","agent_id":"a0a9e7473e236e863","session_id":"b44fd475-ac61-4f5f-bd96-ddf2677bf168","uuid":"15279e13-979c-4c69-b165-ae5481103560","timestamp":"2026-10-22T16:56:46.044Z","subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Review RHAISTRAT-9011 decomposition"}
